#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125504} :activity/id #uuid "f93f830f-ca1a-5ca2-a1ee-45fe0506d2e7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050259 :topic/id #uuid "3a3e1a52-75c9-5495-b430-05a58e4d5dde" :topic/math-academy-id 1873} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125315 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124467 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124271 :question/id #uuid "f0e213c5-8a7a-51a3-995f-394270a22bd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-3559"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cfbdc8f-6c4f-5411-b6f5-b7f0f046ac4f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050255}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545871}] :knowledge-point/title "The Limit at Infinity for a Bounded Function"} :step/id #uuid "722ddd1c-5e61-5fe1-9200-a3d2c15284fb" :step/math-academy-id 655038 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125317}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125317 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125744 :tutorial/content "Not every function $f(x)$ levels off to approach an asymptote as $x$ approaches infinity.\n\nInstead, a function $f(x)$ might get bigger and bigger, increasing without bound, as shown in the graph below.\n\n![](images/99/99d75e971e3ac33723bd6a012806af08ae69a5677a7a44553569bdf6706a81ac.png)\n\nIn this case, we say that the limit of the function is infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x) = \\infty\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c3614f64-54f5-51dd-8908-af9d99f7da31" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2584 :tutorial/title "Infinite Limits"} :step/id #uuid "8f7c3cbf-6b88-5be0-b80b-aa8a91f57ff6" :step/math-academy-id 655042 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125505}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125504 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125714 :tutorial/content "Until now, we have considered limits at individual points: \n\n$\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} f(x)$ is the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ approaches the point $a.$ \n\nHowever, the idea of a limit extends beyond individual points. For instance, it might also be the case that $f(x)$ approaches some value as $x$ gets bigger and bigger, growing without bound.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x} + 1$ whose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/71/71fdfd68ce32097cac17da935f0422b471b28d804f4108c62783d0ffc1e96f24.png)\n\nOn the right side of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets bigger and bigger, $f(x)$ approaches the horizontal asymptote $y=1.$ In other words, as $x$ increases to infinity ($\\infty$), the value of $f(x)$ approaches a limit of $1.$ We can write this symbolically as\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\infty } f(x) = 1.\n$$\n\nLikewise, on the left side of the graph, as $x$ decreases to negative infinity ($-\\infty$), the value of $f(x)$ approaches the same horizontal asymptote $y=1.$ Consequently, we also have that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -\\infty } f(x) = 1.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7f754170-0622-5c95-a522-eab185c0e2d0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 152 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3a7a7874-637c-5af9-9935-68417a30cf66" :step/math-academy-id 655037 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125315}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125505 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124579 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124193 :question/id #uuid "a5050a89-0b53-5499-8a78-9d5d7c5a78c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea4038bf-34f0-58cc-a7b6-6a4c1890570f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545855}] :knowledge-point/title "The Limit at Infinity for an Unbounded Function"} :step/id #uuid "f069f004-01b3-5bd3-845a-6e1c33680f54" :step/math-academy-id 655043}] :activity/title "Limits at Infinity from Graphs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124686}]}
